<!--
  AP session-rx buffer-pool config, SMALL class: the slots a tokio (AP / Linux)
  UDP link's inbound datagrams are received into when they are no larger than
  the datagram the sessions actually send (ARCHITECTURE section 9.2). The LARGE
  class beside it is `session_rx_pool_ap.scxml`, the node's link-RX table that
  the stream read halves fill, whose slot holds the largest frame any AP link
  can present, and so every UDP datagram (65507 bytes at most over IPv4). A
  datagram is received into the smallest class it fits, or the larger one when
  that class has no free slot; a datagram neither class has a slot for is
  refused and counted, never received into an allocation.

  Born with its consumer: `wz-runtime-tokio`'s datagram receive
  (`link_rx_datagram`), behind `runtime-zero-copy`, for the UDP dial read half,
  the accept demux pump and the `UdpDriver` read that multicast group links and
  the scouting socket use. One instance per NODE, as the large class is: the
  datagram links of a node share it.

  ## slot-size 1472 is DERIVED from what a peer's UDP session sends

  The datagrams a UDP session sends in steady state are at most its link mtu:
  `UDP_LINK_MTU` = 1450 on wz, and zenoh-pico's own figure
  (`_z_get_link_mtu_udp_unicast`, the same 1450, and its multicast sibling),
  so that a datagram fits one Ethernet frame without IP fragmentation. A peer
  whose batch is larger sends larger datagrams, and those take the large class.

  The class admits a datagram only when it is STRICTLY shorter than a slot, and
  that byte is the truncation witness rather than slack: the reader learns a
  datagram's length from the socket before it picks a class, and a receive that
  fills the whole slot says the length it was told was short, which a slot of
  exactly the datagram's size could not tell from a datagram of that size. So
  the slot must hold 1450 + 1, and 1451 rounded up to the 64-byte alignment
  below is 1472.

  ## slot-count 258 is DERIVED from the accept demux's own bound

  A UDP listener's pump routes each datagram to its face's channel, whose depth
  is `UDP_DEMUX_FACE_CHANNEL_CAP` = 256: a datagram that finds the channel full
  is dropped, exactly as the kernel's receive buffer drops one when its reader
  falls behind. For that documented drop rule to stay the one that fires for a
  face, the pool must hold one face's full channel, the frame its read half is
  handing to the session and the datagram the pump is receiving: 256 + 1 + 1 =
  258. `link_rx_datagram` asserts this against the constant at compile time.

  Past that the count is not a correctness bound but a throughput figure, as
  `session_rx_pool_ap.scxml` says of its own: the node's datagram links share
  the class, frames the application keeps hold their slots, and a datagram that
  finds both classes out is refused and counted.

  At 258 x 1472 the class is about 380 KiB, allocated zeroed once per node, on
  the first datagram a UDP link receives.

  ## section / alignment / cache-policy

  `heap` / 64 / `none`, as the other AP pools.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="buffer-pool" name="session_rx_pool_ap_small" version="1.0">
  <sce:slot-count>258</sce:slot-count>
  <sce:slot-size>1472</sce:slot-size>
  <sce:section>heap</sce:section>
  <sce:alignment>64</sce:alignment>
  <sce:cache-policy>none</sce:cache-policy>
</scxml>
